A||N||4||FieroSTETZ||how quick are berettas vs. cavaliers?||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||05-14-2000||11:32 AM||||how quick is a chevy baretta vs. a cavalier? and a beretta GT vs a cavalier z34? i am looking to help a friend buy a car, and he wats a fiero, but needs something with 4 seats. he doesnt care about handling, just wants the "small car big sounding engine" type of deal. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||05-14-2000||02:33 PM||||My sister had a '90 3.1 Beretta with an auto.  It was actually pretty snappy.  The Beretta Indy's are VERY quick.  They've got the Quad4.  Never been in a 3.1 cav, but they've got to be close.  The newer style cavalier z's are DOHC 4-bangers.  150hp if I remember correctly. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||05-14-2000||03:01 PM||||If it makes any difference, the Cavs were the Z<B>2</B>4 and the Luminas were the Z<B>3</B>4.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BN Boomer||05-14-2000||09:30 PM||||My wife has a Pontiac Sunfire, the Cavalier's sibling, with the Twin Cam 2.4L, aka quad 4, and a 4 speed auto. I's actually a pretty zippy little car. I've often wondered how that engine and tranny would do in the Fiero. Maybe one of these days I'll do some informal time tests with her car.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Gold-86SE||05-15-2000||12:23 PM||||I have a '92 Sunbird SE 3.1L V6 5-speed since 1993, I am the second owner and it has been a very good driver for the last 210k miles.  As far as I know, the Cavalier/Sunbird (the GM J-Body) are the same cars (engine and drivetrain, only things that are different is your appearance).<P><BR>Here is a little comparision for you.<P>The 3.1 V6 is quick considering the size of the J-Body, mine is currently down for maintenance but was very reliable until I picked up my Fiero in January.<P>My brother at one time owned a '93 or '94 automatic 3.1 V6 Beretta (not a GT) and that thing kept up with my Sunbird. I had to drive mine for everything it was worth just to keep up with that automatic.  Now granted mine, at the time, was 150k older than his Beretta, still the Beretta was a hot little number.<P>During my days, I have also owned a Beretta GT, an '88 2.8 V6 automatic.  The car drove well and handled nicely, but the V6's were underpowered until the new 3.1 came out around 1992.<P>I have enjoyed both my Sunbird and my Beretta and the newer Berettas are quick, for a family car.  A V6 Sunbird/Cavalier Beretta/Corsica will not leave you guessing while in traffic, it sounds good and performs well, even under strong demand.<P>If you are trying to persuade him to the Beretta, have him pick up a newer model. I believe '95 or '96 was the last year made.  From about late '93 until the end of production the Beretta/Corisca had rear wheel independant suspension, instead of a solid rear axle, this improved the ride and handling greatly.  (Please, someone verify for me the beginning date of the rear wheel independant suspension on the Beretta).<P>If interior leg room is a factor in determining the car of choice, the Sunbird has LESS interior room and LESS leg room than the Beretta. The J-Body is a slightly smaller platform that the Corsica/Beretta.  But you would be amazed at the things you can cram into the trunk of either platform.<P>That is my $.02.<P><BR>Had some mis-spellings in my letter.<p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 05-15-2000).]||||reg||6
